<h2 id="when-to-use">When to use this pattern</h2>

<p>Use a service unavailable page when your service cannot be used temporarily or permanently.</p>
<p>A service might close temporarily because of:</p>
<ul>
  <li>unplanned problems which will take time to fix</li>
  <li>reasons outside of your control such as a hosting provider outage</li>
  <li>planned maintenance or upgrades</li>
</ul>
<p>If there's an unexpected problem that you cannot fix quickly, use this pattern. "Quickly" means different things for different services, but for example, within an hour.</p>
<p>Have a general page in case you need to make a service unavailable quickly and do not have time to update the page. As soon as you know when the service will be available, update the page and let users know.</p>
<p>Consider what users should do while the service is unavailable. For example, include a phone number they can contact for urgent issues or a link to a form they can download to record information temporarily.</p>
<p>There are different versions of this pattern:</p>
<ul>
  <li><a href="#general-page">general page</a></li>
  <li><a href="#standalone-page">standalone page</a></li>
  <li><a href="#updates">when you have updates about availability</a></li>
  <li><a href="#if-you-are-planning-to-make-your-service-unavailable">if you are planning to make your service unavailable</a></li>
  <li><a href="#if-you-can-tell-people-when-the-service-will-open-again">if you can tell people when the service will open again</a></li>
  <li><a href="#service-closed-permanently">service closed permanently</a></li>
</ul>

<h2 id="when-not-to-use">When not to use this pattern</h2>
<p>If there is an unexpected problem that you can fix quickly, use the <a href="/design-system/patterns/problem-with-this-service-page">There is a problem with this service page</a>.</p>
<p>Use a <a href="/design-system/patterns/page-not-found-page">page not found</a> if someone is trying to view a page that does not exist.</p>

<h2 id="how-to-use">How to use this pattern</h2>
<p>The page should include:</p>
<ul> 
  <li>"Sorry, this online service is currently unavailable – service name – NHS" as the page title or if it's permanent: "Sorry, this online service is now closed – service name – NHS"</li>
  <li>"Sorry, this online service is currently unavailable" as the <code>&lt;h1&gt;</code> or if it's permanent: "Sorry, this online service is now closed"</li>
  <li>the day, date and time it is going to be available or what to do if it is permanently closed</li>
  <li>an alternative journey for users on a transactional service</li>
  <li>contact information if it helps meet a user need</li>
</ul>
<p>This is one of the rare times when we use the word "sorry". That's because it is a serious problem for the user and it's our fault.</p>
<p>If your users are trying to complete a task, such as registering for something, offer them an alternative way to do it. For example, another way to complete a form. Consider the options with your clinical team.</p>
<p>Include contact information if it meets a user need or helps them complete their task. For example, if someone wants to cancel an appointment. Contact information should include all numbers and opening times or link to a specific page that includes numbers and opening times. If it's helpful, include "If you need urgent medical advice, use <a href="#">111 online</a> or call 111."</p>
<p>If you can:</p>
<ul>
  <li>store previously entered information for a reasonable amount of time so users can resume their journey with re-populated information when the service becomes available again</li>
  <li>tell the user what has happened to their answers if they're in the middle of a transaction</li>
</ul>
<p>Do not ask the public to report errors, unless you know that this meets a user need. Users of internal systems and services may want to report issues.</p>
<p>Have clear and concise content and do not use:</p>
<ul>
  <li>breadcrumbs or a back button</li>
  <li>vague, unhelpful words like "improvements"</li>
  <li>red text to warn people</li>
  <li>too many links</li>
</ul>

